# Cloudflare Pages _headers file — applied to all responses.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/

/*
  # Security headers (OWASP baseline for static site)
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  # No inline scripts except our own (set:html blocks for JSON-LD) — unsafe-inline needed for that.
  # EVERY analytics origin the site loads MUST be listed here or the browser silently blocks it.
  # This bit us hard (fixed 2026-07-25): googletagmanager/google-analytics were missing, so GA4
  # was blocked and daily users read ~2/day from Jul 19 while Clarity independently measured ~790
  # humans/3d — a ~40x under-report on one of the fleet's few trafficked sites. It only surfaced on
  # Jul 19 because the Jul-18 switch to `wrangler pages deploy` (commit 9af8588) is what started
  # actually SHIPPING this _headers file; the CSP had been written before GA4 was ever added.
  # Verified live: gtag/js returned HTTP 503 (CSP block) while clarity.ms — which IS listed — 200'd.
  # script-src  = where a <script src> may load FROM (gtag loader, CF beacon)
  # connect-src = where JS may SEND to (the /g/collect beacon; GA4 also uses region1.* endpoints)
  # If you add an analytics/ads vendor later, add it to BOTH or it will fail the same silent way.
  #
  # 2026-07-30 — that warning came true. public/amazon-track.js has called
  # navigator.sendBeacon('https://fleet.promptprio.com/c?s=colorcombinations.org') since
  # 2026-07-19 (commit e289b06), but fleet.promptprio.com was never added here. sendBeacon
  # is governed by connect-src, so the browser silently refused EVERY Amazon-click beacon.
  # Cost: Amazon's own Tracking-ID report shows 238 clicks for colorcombinations-20 over
  # Jun 30–Jul 29, while the fleet metrics layer recorded 1 — so the dashboard rated this
  # site 0.1% click-rate (near-worst) when Amazon-side it is 238/1,646 = 14.5%, the BEST
  # converter in the fleet (readinglist is 3.1%). A measurement bug, not a conversion
  # problem, and it was actively misdirecting where work went. sendBeacon needs only
  # connect-src (nothing is loaded FROM there), so script-src is deliberately unchanged.
  #
  # 2026-09-04 — Mediavine ad network added. Third time this file decides whether a
  # vendor works or silently does nothing, so the reasoning is written out.
  #
  # The tag is: <script src="//scripts.scriptwrapper.com/tags/0e3765cf-...js">
  # scripts.scriptwrapper.com was NOT in the old script-src, so the browser would have
  # refused to load it: the tag sits in the HTML, every grep "passes", the site looks
  # perfect, and zero ads ever render. frame-src had no entry at all and fell back to
  # default-src 'self', which blocks every ad IFRAME — i.e. every actual ad — and
  # media-src likewise blocked video ads. Both are now explicit.
  #
  # Why the ad directives are `https:` and not a host allowlist. Programmatic ads are
  # not enumerable: the wrapper loads Google Ad Manager, then a rotating set of prebid
  # adapters (Magnite, PubMatic, Xandr, Index, Criteo, OpenX, Amazon TAM...), and the
  # creatives render in iframes served from ARBITRARY advertiser domains. Mediavine
  # changes partners without telling publishers. A hand-written allowlist is therefore
  # guaranteed to go stale and to fail the SAME SILENT WAY as the two incidents above —
  # no error, no console line anyone will see, just less revenue with no signal saying
  # why. A partial allowlist is worse than none: it looks like protection and behaves
  # like a slow leak.
  #
  # What that costs in security, honestly: less than it looks. script-src has carried
  # 'unsafe-inline' from the start (JSON-LD via set:html), and 'unsafe-inline' already
  # lets any HTML injection execute script. The host list only ever stopped external
  # FILES from unlisted origins; it never stopped the inline path. The loss is bounded
  # by default-src 'self' — every directive NOT named below is still same-origin only.
  #
  # Deliberately NOT relaxed, because these still do real work:
  #   frame-ancestors 'none' — clickjacking (with X-Frame-Options: DENY above)
  #   base-uri 'self'        — stops <base> injection rewriting every relative URL
  #   form-action 'self' ... — stops a form being repointed at an attacker
  #   object-src 'none'      — NEW; strictly tighter than the old default-src fallback
  # Previously-named analytics origins (kept here for a future tightening, now covered
  # by https:): news.google.com, *.clarity.ms, googletagmanager, *.google-analytics.com,
  # *.analytics.google.com, static.cloudflareinsights.com, fleet.promptprio.com.
  # 2026-09-05 — `blob:` added to img-src. Fourth time this file decides whether a
  # feature works or silently does nothing, so: /tools/palette-from-image/ (an indexed
  # sitemap URL) does `const url = URL.createObjectURL(f); img.src = url` to read pixels
  # off a canvas. img-src was `'self' data: https:` with no blob:, so the browser refused
  # the object URL, img.onload never fired, and the tool sat there doing nothing — no
  # error a user would ever see. worker-src already carried blob:, so the omission was an
  # inconsistency rather than a decision. Scope kept minimal deliberately: ONLY img-src
  # changes, because createObjectURL on an <img> is the only blob: use actually verified
  # in the source. media-src / connect-src are NOT widened on speculation. The /embed/*
  # block below is untouched — it has no blob: need and stays tighter.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https:; font-src 'self' data: https:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; media-src 'self' https:; frame-src https:; worker-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https:; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self' https://forms.example.com

# Embed widgets — deliberately iframe-embeddable on any origin.
# Overrides the global X-Frame-Options: DENY + frame-ancestors 'none'
# so third-party blogs, Notion pages, design reviews etc. can embed
# palette + collection previews via <iframe>. Without this block, the
# /embed/* routes return 200 but browsers refuse to render them in a frame.
/embed/*
  ! X-Frame-Options
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors *; base-uri 'self'
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, s-maxage=3600

# Long-cache for hashed assets (Astro emits .hash.css, .hash.js)
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Long-cache for static images
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate

/og-default.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate

# Sitemap + robots should be freshly crawled
/sitemap-index.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
/sitemap-0.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
